g this from kasir  not directory from plato man is declared  to be that creature who's constantly in  search of himself a creature who in  every moment of his existence must  examine and scrutinize the conditions of  his existence and then here we get the  quote he is a being in search of meaning  so we have a rather existentialist plato  here which is to say not the plato that  we know from his actual texts  this is another common one that you see  floating around quite a bit atheism is a  disease of the soul before it becomes an  error of the understanding and this is  coming from what we could call a  mistaken attribution a summary that's  being given of what plato is supposed to  be saying but not actually coming from  plato's text and it's attributed to  plato's laws by this author william  fleming and then it gets quoted by  somebody else who's taking william  fleming at his word in prose quotations  from socrates to macaulay by samuel  austin alibone so fleming is claiming  that this is what plato says in the laws  and you can't really find anything  corresponding directly to this in it so  it seems to be fleming's misremembering  or misattribution that gets this going  and this becomes a real darling among  conservatives you can find it for  example in conservapedia and in other  places where it's being regurgitated  if you know your ancient philosophy well  then as soon as you see this you're most  likely going to say no that's not plato  that's actually  clearly aristotle although it's not  actually what aristotle  says and it's kind of a weird  translation as well and so we'll see why  in just a minute so excellence is not a  gift but a skill that takes practice we  do not act rightly because we are  excellent in fact we achieve excellence  by acting rightly now if you know you're  greek you would say okay excellence  virtue you know arete great that's fine  a skill  well  that sounds like techne and that's not  something that plato or aristotle  actually